Cutscenes, radio chatter, and strong characters make this the most intimate Ace Combat yet.
How Ace Combat 8: Wings of Theve uses first-person to tell a more personal story
The world of Strangereal, setting of the Ace Combat games, has developed a deep and storied history over the course of the series. But with Ace Combat 8: Wings of Theve , developer Project Aces has changed its approach to storytelling to create a more intimate and emotional tale than it has in the past. That approach brings you a lot closer to the desperate attempts of the Federation of Central Usea&#8217;s battered navy to fight off the invading Sotoans, and the many characters fighting that war.
The biggest change is that, in Ace Combat 8, you play a fighter pilot silent protagonist who is, nevertheless, a major character throughout the story. The game opens with a cutscene in which you&#8217;re found floating in a life raft after a battle by the Endurance, one of the FCU&#8217;s last remaining aircraft carriers.&nbsp;
After you&#8217;re aboard, you&#8217;re sent up as navigator for fighter pilot Jan &#8220;Rex&#8221; Cope, whose job isn&#8217;t to engage in battles, but to serve as a propaganda tool. He&#8217;s known as the Wings of Theve, a legendary fight pilot ace named for the FCU&#8217;s capital, but you quickly find out that Cope isn&#8217;t the incredible ace he&#8217;s portrayed to be on social media. Instead, his instructions are to run from every fight, but when the two of you are shot down, Cope is killed in action.&nbsp;
But the Wings of Theve is too important to the struggling FCU to go out that way, so you inherit Cope&#8217;s call sign and secretly replace him. As the new Wings of Theve, you have a central role in the war effort, and your skills help bolster the propaganda — for better or worse.
Previous Ace Combat games have told their stories through detailed cutscenes, but in Ace Combat 8, those cutscenes are mostly presented from a first-person perspective, putting you in a room or on the deck with other characters. The story unfolds as if you&#8217;re living it both in and out of the cockpit, rather than having it narrated to you, and haptic feedback in the DualSense controller helps make those moments feel realistic and intense, especially in the heat of battle.
Ace Combat 8 puts a big emphasis on all its characters, especially the members of Joker Flight. You spend almost the whole game with them, whether it&#8217;s hanging out during cutscenes between missions that expand on the plot, sitting through briefings where they&#8217;ll chime in with comments or information, or listening to them over the radio while you&#8217;re taking down enemy aircraft.&nbsp;
